# Images uploaded through the page editor live here, and they must be SERVED —
# so unlike /data this directory is readable. What must never happen is one of
# them being EXECUTED: an upload is attacker-supplied content, and a directory
# that both accepts files and runs code is the classic way a site is taken over.
#
# upload_image.php already re-encodes everything through GD, so what lands here
# is always a real JPG and never source code. This is the second lock on the
# same door: belt and braces, because the cost is four lines and the failure is
# total.
<FilesMatch "\.(php|phtml|php[0-9]|phar|cgi|pl|py|sh|htaccess)$">
    Require all denied
</FilesMatch>
# Guarded: php_flag exists only under mod_php. On a server running PHP-FPM —
# which most now do — an unguarded php_flag is a fatal configuration error and
# would take the whole site down with a 500. The FilesMatch above is the rule
# that actually does the work; this only helps where it applies.
<IfModule mod_php7.c>
    php_flag engine off
</IfModule>
<IfModule mod_php.c>
    php_flag engine off
</IfModule>
